WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Good morning, John. So you know how I'm not really a huge fan

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.920
of, like, the institution of advertising,

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.100
but then I also built an entire business on advertising revenue

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:11.240
so that we can make things like SciShow and Crash Course and all the other cool stuff that we do?

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:12.380
So that was a weird decision for me.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:17.140
It's actually not, because YouTube provides two things that really nobody else does.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:20.640
One, a truly massive audience that, if you create quality content

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.220
and/or have a strategy that matches well with what YouTube wants,

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.600
YouTube might push toward your content.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:30.220
And two, a system to turn those views...into dollars.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:31.220
REAL LIFE MONEY.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:35.380
So until there's someone who's doing those two things better than YouTube is, no one will ever leave YouTube.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:39.240
Like, Facebook is just doing ONE of those things, and people are posting their content there like crazy.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.020
YouTube has been sharing revenue with content creators for 10 years,

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.200
during which time Facebook has just been, like, "Meh."

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:48.580
So this has created an amazing ecosystem of creative people doing cool things on this platform

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:51.780
who can make money--y'know, maybe not a TON, but without a lot of HEADACHE, y'know?

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:53.820
Here's the crazy thing about YouTube advertising, right?

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:58.200
Every ad that plays on a YouTube video, that person is getting paid, like, ten times less

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.600
than if that ad played on TV.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.980
And for every minute of content watched,

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:05.760
there's, like, five times fewer ads.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.940
So the average YouTube channel gets, like, FIFTY TIMES less revenue

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.420
than the average TV channel per minute of viewership.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.840
WHYYY?! Why are my eyeballs worth ten times more when I'm watching TV

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:17.600
than when I'm watching YouTube? THEY'RE THE SAME EYEBALLS, I promise.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:22.380
There HAS to be a point at which, like, your audience is being valued SO LOW

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:24.400
by these people that you're just, like, "NO!"

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.440
Here's the big secret about YouTube ads:

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:27.520
They're YouTube's whole business,

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.920
and it's a BIG business. It's billions of dollars.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.100
But it's precarious.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:34.220
Ads...are just worth less on the internet.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:37.620
People don't like them, people skip them, they block them, they get FRUSTRATED by them,

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:40.900
and while my eyeballs, for whatever reason, seem to be worth less on the internet,

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.640
my TIME is worth MORE.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.800
I'm BUSY! This is not couch time, this is ACTIVE time!

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:49.000
So this weird situation is where we're at, when, a few weeks ago,

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.780
uh, some inflammatory articles are posted about how major brands

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:56.820
are being advertised on top of...very VILE YouTube videos.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:01.200
A bunch of advertisers, fearing backlash, remove their ads ENTIRELY from YouTube.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.000
And during this period, EVERY YouTuber saw a decrease in revenue.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.180
Also, Google's valuation dropped by, like, seven-hundred-and-fifty million dollars in a day.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:09.620
NO BIG! It's FINE!

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:12.300
Now, YouTube has a few different systems that put content into buckets.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.980
They have a manual bucket that's, like, hand-picked channels, like Crash Course,

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:19.580
where they can say, "This is good! Advertisers, it's safe, go here, it's very expensive."

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:23.580
And then they have an algorithmic bucket that's, like, "No ads on any of this stuff ever,

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:26.000
because it's about marijuana smoking, or... whatever."

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.180
And then there's a middle area, where it's confusing and no one knows how it works at all.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:33.180
But what we DO know is after this hullabaloo, all of the algorithms got TIGHTENED.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:36.640
So while some channels have rebounded, a lot of channels went down,

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:40.720
and then went down FURTHER, as they were put into buckets which weren't getting as many ads.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.000
This has really squeezed creators who are making content that's maybe GOOD,

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.600
but not, like, super-happy-family-fun-time stuff.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:49.100
And again, it's like, "Is this worth it?"

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.800
Is this relationship between content and brands (that was designed, by the way, for RADIO),

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.800
is it a useless holdover from another century?

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:58.200
And if so, are we going to have to find other ways to turn  the value that we create

00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.860
into value that we can spend?

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:03.660
And if so, will advertisers lose their ability to reach

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.100
people who are under the age of sixty-five?

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.180
And if so, is that really a bad thing

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:10.980
because do we really NEED advertisers telling us how to spend our money?

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:12.920
I feel like- I feel like I could figure that out on my own.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:15.520
If we're forced to, if the value proposition breaks,

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:18.220
we will find, and we already have started to do this,

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:22.420
new ways to support great content on the internet.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:25.000
Subscription services, like Rooster Teeth's "First,"

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:29.600
YouTube RED, Patreon, really great and productive relationships,

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:32.900
like, one-on-one relationships between creators and brands that communities LIKE,

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:36.420
as this continues to happen, YouTube and advertisers are gonna have to be careful

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:41.700
because eventually, they're gonna need our eyeballs more than we need their pennies.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:43.580
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:44.380
(dramatic silence)

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:46.080
If you are a creator who makes stuff on the internet,

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.940
the Internet Creators' Guild is a thing that is designed to advocate for people like you.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:52.920
And if you're interested in finding out more about that,

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:54.025
there's some links in the description.

